.TH HU_RESOLVE 3 "" "helios-unix" "Library functions"
.SH NAME
hu_resolve_launch, hu_resolve_receive, hu_resolve_send \- run name lookup in a helper task
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <helios/unix/resolve.h>

int hu_resolve_launch(pid_t *pid, int writer, const char *name,
                      int reverse, const struct hu_spawn_attr *attr);
int hu_resolve_receive(int reader, struct hu_resolve_result *result);
int hu_resolve_send(int writer, const char *name, int reverse);

struct hu_resolve_result {
    int error, count;
    unsigned char addresses[32][4];
    char canonical[256];
};
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Use a native channel and a separate resolver task so that a slow DNS query
does not block the main application's event loop.
.IP 1. 3
Create endpoints with hu_channel.
.IP 2.
Pass the write endpoint to hu_resolve_launch. It starts /helios/lib/hu-resolve
with that endpoint as stdout. name must contain 1..253 bytes. A nonzero
reverse requests reverse-name lookup as well. attr may select task placement;
the helper always closes extra descriptors at or above 3.
.IP 3.
On success, close the parent's write endpoint and save the PID. On launch
failure, close both endpoints. The launch call does not consume either one.
.IP 4.
Wait for input on the read endpoint using hu_poll and an application-selected
query deadline. Then call hu_resolve_receive. After successful receipt, check
result.error before using its count, network-order address bytes or canonical
name. A successful transport does not imply successful DNS.
.IP 5.
Close the reader and reap the helper with waitpid. On cancellation or timeout,
close the channel, terminate the helper with kill, and reap it. Handle EINTR
when waiting for the child.
.PP
hu_resolve_receive sets the reader nonblocking and leaves it that way. It may
wait up to two seconds to assemble a fragmented reply; this is not the DNS
query deadline. A partial-reply failure is terminal: cancel and clean up
rather than calling receive again on the consumed frame.
.PP
hu_resolve_send is the helper-side synchronous lookup and reply writer.
Applications normally use launch instead. The fixed 392-byte message contains
values, never pointers or shared process state. Results have no dynamically
allocated members and need no free function.
.SH RETURN VALUE
hu_resolve_launch returns 0 or an error number, following hu_spawn.
hu_resolve_receive and hu_resolve_send return 0 or -1 with errno set.
receive reports EIO for malformed or truncated replies and ETIMEDOUT for a
stalled reply. After receive returns 0, result.error is an EAI code, described
by gai_strerror. count is at most 32; canonical is NUL-terminated.
.SH FILES
/helios/lib/hu-resolve must be installed for launch to work.
.SH SEE ALSO
getaddrinfo(3), hu_channel(3), hu_poll(3), hu_spawn(3)
